three different transformers depending on the data characteristics:

1. MinMaxScaler:
   This transformer rescales numerical features to a fixed range, typically [0, 1].
   It is useful for models that rely on distance calculations, such as KNN or SVM with RBF,
   and it preserves the original shape of the distribution. However, it is sensitive to outliers.

2. StandardScaler:
   This transformer standardizes features by removing the mean and scaling to unit variance.
   It is appropriate when using linear models or gradient-based algorithms because they assume
   features on similar scales. It helps optimization converge faster. However, it does not
   correct skewed distributions.

3. PowerTransformer (Yeo–Johnson):
   This transformer applies a nonlinear transformation that reduces skewness and makes the
   distribution more Gaussian-like. It stabilizes variance and can improve performance for
   linear models. Unlike Box–Cox, Yeo–Johnson supports negative values. It is useful when
   numerical features are highly skewed.
